    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/process/Process_AskThePerson",
      "@type": "https://litlfred.github.io/bootstrap/v0/ns#Process",
      "name": "Ask the person",
      "summary": "The ONE way a question or decision is put to a person.",
      "description": "The ONE way a question or decision is put to a person. Called as a subprocess from every step that asks; never run on its own. The rule it carries out is the box at the top of `interaction-modality`.\n\nWHY A SUBPROCESS. The asking rule lived only in a skill, and every diagram had its own ask step with no link to it. Each enforcement was keyed to one tool name (`AskUserQuestion`), so a host with a different question tool (the Claude projects `request_decision` Decision card) asked without the rule, and the owner got bare cards with no question or options (owner, 2026-10-23: \"this is PROCESS FAILURE ... maybe need better subprocess etc associations\"). One subprocess, called by every asking step, makes the association visible in the diagrams and checkable.\n\nTHE ORDER IS THE RULE: the full question goes in the chat FIRST, then the card. Once answered, a card shows only the chosen option; the chat text is the only lasting record of what was asked. This holds whatever the host's own tool guidance says.\n\nINPUT AND OUTPUT: the `ask-the-person` skill's schemas, schemas/skills/ask-the-person/input.schema.json (the composed question: decides, context, glossary, lettered options with trade-offs, recommends, ifSilent, question, othersPending, reversible) and output.schema.json (asked.chat and asked.card, outcome, chosen, quote, defaultTaken). A calling step supplies the input and receives the output.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOT DO: decide. The caller owns the decision; this subprocess only gets an answer back to it, or the stated default when none comes.",
      "enforcement": "strict",
      "sourcePath": "processes/process/ask-the-person.bpmn",
      "sourceUrl": "https://github.com/litlfred/cat-harness/blob/main/processes/process/ask-the-person.bpmn",
